---
title: Housing Lower Manhattan
authors: [Torben Karl]
broadcast: 2
order: 1
start: "4:22"
---

**1.** The first New Yorker's drank from a 48-acre freshwater source they named Collect Pond. It was drained and filled by the city (from 1802-1813) The fill was cheap, unstable, and sank.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-01.jpg" alt="Map of the Collect Pond and the streets around it in Lower Manhattan" /%}

**2.** This unstable ground became the Five Points Neighborhood, bounded by Centre Street, the Bowery, Canal Street, and Park Row. The land was cheap, and would become home to poor Irish, German, Italian, Black, and later Chinese New Yorkers through the 1800s.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-02.jpg" alt="Painting of a crowded street corner at Five Points" /%}

**3.** The word "Tenement" was legally defined in 1867 as any building with 3 or more families sharing plumbing. In 1867, this definition was revised, requiring one fire escape per unit, one privy per 20 tenants, and at least one window per room. In 1879, "Old Law" stipulated that windows must face open air, encouraging a narrow air shaft between buildings. Thus the "dumbbell tenement" was born, with a gently pinched waist. Once the airshafts had filled with garbage and stagnant water, the 1901 "New Law" required a full courtyard instead of a shaft.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-03.jpg" alt="Photograph looking down a narrow air shaft between tenement roofs" /%}

**4.** Jacob Riis was a Danish immigrant, who arrived in New York in 1870. For over a decade he documented tenement life in the Lower East Side as a police reporter and in 1890, he published "How the Other Half Lives." In it, Riis focused on Mulberry Bend, which he called the "foul core of New York's slums." Riis forced tenement conditions into mainstream reform politics, and Mulberry Bend was demolished just seven years later.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-04.jpg" alt="Jacob Riis photograph of people sleeping in a crowded tenement room" /%}

**5.** Chinese immigrants settled around Mott, Pell, and Doyers Streets from the 1870s, next to the Five Points neighborhood. The Chinese Exclusion Act (1882) barred Chinese laborers from immigrating or naturalizing and barred wives and children of laborers already here from joining them. By 1900, about 7,000 Chinese men lived in Manhattan against fewer than 200 women. Neither banks nor outside landlords would deal with the Chinese community, so "bachelor apartments," were arranged through family and district associations, often occupying previous tenements. This self-built network of mutual aid long outlasted Exclusion.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-05.jpg" alt="Photograph of men sharing a meal at a table in a Chinatown apartment" /%}

**6.** Once word was out on how the other half lived, Knickerbocker Village came as an extreme over-correction. Developer Fred F. French assembled 14+ acres of an Italian immigrant enclave named the "Lung Block" (for its extreme tuberculosis rates). In 1933, French secured the country's first federal loan for slum-clearance housing, through the Reconstruction Finance Corporation (RFC). RFC covered about 97% of the $10 million cost. The project included 12 thirteen-story buildings, about 1,600 apartments. Rents were unsurprisingly about 2.5x what Lung Block tenants paid ($5/room to about $12.50/room). This priced out nearly all of the original ~ 650 displaced families. New tenants were mostly white-collar workers.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-06.jpg" alt="Aerial photograph of the Knickerbocker Village blocks" /%}

**7.** After Exclusion was repealed in 1943, strict quotas were kept on the community until the Immigration and Nationality Act of 1965 ended national-origin quotas and opened family reunification. Population surged from roughly 20,000 Chinese New Yorkers in the mid-1960s to roughly 150,000 within about two decades. New arrivals moved into the same old-law tenements built for the earlier bachelor generation and overcrowding intensified. The garment trade grew alongside it and by the 1980s it employed a large share of Chinatown residents. Factories operated on the same floors people slept on, even in the same rooms.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-07.jpg" alt="Photograph of a woman at a sewing machine at home, with a child beside her" /%}

**8.** When, in 1977, Charles Jencks declared the demolition of Minoru Yamasaki's Pruitt-Igoe in St. Louis the "death of modern architecture," public opinion on tower- block housing was flipped from "solution" to a symbol of Modernist "failure." Of course, the housing project's rapid decline through the 1960s was due to things much greater than architectural form - racial segregation, disinvestment, underfunded maintenance, white flight from St. Louis. What is the responsibility of a building's form? How is this cautionary tale retold in the Big Apple?

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-08.jpg" alt="Photograph of the demolition of a Pruitt-Igoe tower" /%}

**9.** Two housing projects were initiated by the Association for Middle Income Housing that defined a brutalist vision for housing residents of Lower Manhattan. Chatham Green (165 Park Row), which held 450 units in a long serpentine block, and Chatham Towers (170/180 Park Row) two 25-story towers with 250 co-op units. Both projects answer the same "slum" condition that produced Five Points a century earlier by clearing the tenement fabric outright and replacing it with modernist towers-in-a-plaza, rather than reforming it in place the way the Tenement House Acts had tried to. Today, the original "middle income" co-ops now sell at market rate, with recent one-bedroom listings near $1,000,000.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-09.jpg" alt="Photograph of a Chinatown street with Chatham Towers behind" /%}

**10.** In 1974, construction began on Confucius Plaza at Bowery and Division Street, the first major publicly funded housing built for a Chinese American community in the United States, financed largely through the state's Mitchell-Lama program. The contractor initially declined to hire any Chinese or other Asian workers, and activists organized as Asian Americans for Equal Employment picketed the site for months, carrying signs that asked why the workers who had built the transcontinental railroad could not build Confucius Plaza. Joined eventually by the Black and Puerto Rican Coalition, the protest secured several dozen positions for Asian American workers on the project, and gave rise to an organization, later renamed Asian Americans for Equality, that continues to develop affordable housing in the neighborhood. The building opened in 1975 with seven hundred and sixty-two units, most of them moderate-income cooperatives, and a statue of Confucius standing at its entrance.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-10.jpg" alt="Photograph of a protest against discrimination in the construction industry, Chinatown" /%}

**11.** The plaza in front of that statue has since hosted a different kind of protest. In March 1995, an NYPD officer in Sheepshead Bay, Brooklyn, fatally shot sixteen-year-old Yong Xin Huang while he played with a pellet gun in a friend's backyard; a grand jury declined to indict him. That July, on the eve of what would have been Huang's seventeenth birthday, the Committee Against Anti-Asian Violence led a procession from a Chinatown elementary school to Columbus Park and on to a memorial at Confucius Plaza, where mourners held cardboard tombstones bearing the names of other Asian American victims of police and gun violence, and leaders from the Asian, Black, and Latino communities denounced both the killing and the district attorney's inaction. The plaza that Chinatown had won through a labor protest twenty years earlier became, in this instance, ground for protest of another order entirely.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-11.jpg" alt="Photograph of a protest in Chinatown behind a police barrier" /%}

**12.** In 2012, the Pathmark supermarket on the waterfront, one of the neighborhood's few full-size, affordable grocery stores closed. Community members protested its closing, anticipating that the site would pass to luxury development. Extell's eighty-story One Manhattan Square was completed in 2019 and became the first supertall tower on the Two Bridges waterfront.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-12.jpg" alt="Rendering of the Two Bridges towers on the East River waterfront" /%}

**13.** Three further towers followed close behind, proposed in 2016 under a 1972 zoning designation that permitted the city to approve large developments without full council review. In 2018, the City Planning Commission described these towers as a minor modification of that earlier permit, thereby bypassing the land-use review that would have given the local council member leverage over their height and affordable-housing provisions. The community resisted: the City Council and the Manhattan borough president sued over the bypassed review; tenant and arts organizations filed a second suit and collected thousands of signatures; rallies outside the courthouse chanted "no towers, no compromise." A judge ruled twice in the community's favor, halting construction, just for an appellate court to reverse both decisions, and for the state's highest court to decline a final appeal in 2021.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-13.jpg" alt="Photograph of a CAAAV rally against the Two Bridges towers" /%}

**14.** Lower Manhattan's newest house is being built two blocks from Montez Press Radio's new building, on the site of the demolished Tombs. The city has been building a new Manhattan Detention Center as part of its broader plan to dissolve Rikers Island and at roughly three hundred feet, the tower would be the tallest jail in the world. Neighbors United Below Canal sued the city, and a judge annulled the project's approvals in 2020, only for an appeals court to reinstate them the following year. Protests continued regardless, staged outside the construction site and outside the MoCA, which had accepted thirty-five million dollars from the city as a "community give-back" (more bluntly, "jail money"). In 2025, NUBC proposed an alternative plan: relocating the jail into a nearby federal detention facility that is already standing vacant, and building 1040 units of affordable housing instead. Construction on the jail proceeds regardless, beginning in January 2026, and its budget has grown to nearly 3.8 billion dollars. Its completion is projected for 2032.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-14.jpg" alt="Rendering of the new Manhattan detention center among the surrounding buildings" /%}

**15.** *If this history has a single provisional lesson, it is that nothing built on this ground has stayed settled for long.*

*What separates past interventions that held from the ones that didn't was rarely the strength of an argument on its own. It was whether the people affected kept some kind of leverage over what happened next. The projects that presented themselves as final (a pure vision) were, almost without exception, the ones later generations had to fight over again, at greater cost and usually from a weaker, more fractured, position than the first time.*

*As Montez Press Radio begins its new chapter on this same ground, it might reasonably take up that same posture as a design instruction. What if we idealize space that is flexible, absorbent, chameleon, or extendable? What if we provide too many outlets, faucets, and coat hooks? Would that be enough to absorb the complaints, protests, and renegotiations that are certainly still to come?*

*A space built to be redrawn might, instead, actually last.*

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/torben-15.jpg" alt="Photograph of three people standing on a fire escape" /%}
